<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
    <version>3.5.16</version>
    <relativePath/>
  </parent>

  <groupId>com.finora</groupId>
  <artifactId>finora-backend</artifactId>
  <version>0.2.0</version>
  <name>finora-backend</name>
  <description>Finora Personal Financial Operating System - Backend API</description>

  <properties>
    <!-- Coupled to the parent version above: raising this without raising Spring Boot does not
         work, and fails in two places that both look like application faults.

         Every library that reads or rewrites bytecode has to recognise the class file version
         javac emits (Java 25 is 69), and each one refuses an unknown format rather than guessing.
         At Boot 3.3.2, whose managed versions all predate Java 25:

           Byte Buddy 1.14.18, under Mockito 5.11.0, fails every @Mock at setUp with
           "Mockito cannot mock this class ... Java : 25", i.e. 762 errors across 61 test classes.

           Spring Framework 6.1's bundled ASM fails component scanning outright with
           "Incompatible class format in file [...]", i.e. 57 more, in every @SpringBootTest.

         Compilation succeeds throughout, so the build reads as though it got further than it did.

         Do NOT reach for the workaround Spring names in that second message. Setting
         spring.classformat.ignore=true makes the scanner SKIP classes it cannot parse instead of
         failing, so beans silently go unregistered and the suite goes green on an application
         that is missing parts of itself. On this codebase that is the admin surface.

         Boot 3.5.16 ships Spring Framework 6.2 and manages Byte Buddy 1.17.8 / Mockito 5.17.0,
         all of which read class file 69, so no per-library pin is needed here. If java.version
         rises again, check the parent first: the pin belongs on Boot, not on its dependencies. -->
    <java.version>25</java.version>

    <!-- Docker Engine API version Testcontainers negotiates with. Override with
         -Ddocker.api.version=... if you ever need to.

         Without this, every Testcontainers test fails on a current Docker Desktop:

           client version 1.32 is too old. Minimum supported API version is 1.40

         docker-java (bundled with Testcontainers 1.20.1) falls back to API 1.32 when nothing tells
         it otherwise, and Docker Engine 29 raised its floor to 1.40. Note the setting is docker-java's
         `api.version` property, NOT the DOCKER_API_VERSION environment variable, which docker-java
         does not read and which therefore looks like it does nothing.

         1.40 rather than something newer on purpose: it is the exact floor Engine 29 accepts and has
         been supported since Docker 19.03 (2019), so this one value works on both a brand-new engine
         and anything a developer or CI runner is realistically still running. Pinning something newer
         would just move the incompatibility to the other end. -->
    <docker.api.version>1.40</docker.api.version>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <!-- Web -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- API documentation (Swagger UI at /swagger-ui.html, OpenAPI spec at /v3/api-docs)

         Version is coupled to the Spring Framework line the parent brings in, and springdoc is not
         part of Boot's BOM, so nothing raises it for you. 2.6.0 was correct for Boot 3.3.2 (Spring
         6.1) and breaks on Boot 3.5.16 (Spring 6.2): every request to /v3/api-docs returns 500 with

           NoSuchMethodError: 'void org.springframework.web.method.ControllerAdviceBean.<init>(...)'

         The whole test suite stays green through that, because it is a runtime linkage error on one
         endpoint rather than a context failure. That is why OpenApiSpecIT now exists: the upgrade
         that found this was only caught by asking the running app for its spec. Move this version
         whenever the parent's Spring Framework minor moves. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springdoc</groupId>
      <artifactId>springdoc-openapi-starter-webmvc-ui</artifactId>
      <version>2.9.0</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Health check endpoint (/actuator/health) — used by Docker Compose healthchecks
         and referenced as a permitAll route in SecurityConfig. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Persistence -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Migrations -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flywaydb</groupId>
      <artifactId>flyway-database-postgresql</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Caching. See CacheConfig for the named caches and why Caffeine, not Redis, is the right
         amount of engineering for a single-instance deployment. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-cache</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.github.ben-manes.caffeine</groupId>
      <artifactId>caffeine</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Security + JWT -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-security</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
      <artifactId>jjwt-api</artifactId>
      <version>0.13.0</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
      <artifactId>jjwt-impl</artifactId>
      <version>0.13.0</version>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.jsonwebtoken</groupId>
      <artifactId>jjwt-jackson</artifactId>
      <version>0.13.0</version>
      <scope>runtime</scope>
    </dependency>

    <!-- D-23: Sign in with Google (ID token verification, not the Gmail data-integration OAuth
         flow above; see GoogleOAuthProperties' own doc comment on that distinction). Provides
         GoogleIdTokenVerifier: signature verification against Google's published JWKS (with
         correct caching/rotation/clock-skew handling), issuer and audience checks. Reimplementing
         this with the JWT library already used for Finora's own tokens would mean re-deriving
         JWKS fetching/caching and the exact claim-validation rules by hand for a security
         boundary; Google's own audited library is the safer choice here, not the smaller
         dependency. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.google.api-client</groupId>
      <artifactId>google-api-client</artifactId>
      <version>2.9.0</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- D-23 Phase 2: Sign in with Apple (ID token verification). Apple, unlike Google, has no
         first-party Java verification library; google-api-client's GoogleIdTokenVerifier above
         has no Apple equivalent. jwks-rsa is the standard, focused library for exactly the piece
         that's genuinely hard to get right by hand: fetching Apple's published JWKS, resolving the
         right key by `kid`, and caching/rotating them. It hands back a java.security.PublicKey;
         the JWT parsing and signature/claim verification itself is done with jjwt (already a
         dependency, used for Finora's own tokens), same division of labor as Google's verifier
         used above: a small, audited library for key resolution, this codebase's own JWT
         tooling for the rest. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.auth0</groupId>
      <artifactId>jwks-rsa</artifactId>
      <version>0.24.1</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Validation -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
    </dependency>

    <!-- CSV parsing (statement import) -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.opencsv</groupId>
      <artifactId>opencsv</artifactId>
      <version>5.12.0</version>
      <exclusions>
        <!-- opencsv -> commons-beanutils -> commons-logging. Spring Boot already provides
             spring-jcl as the commons-logging replacement (see spring-boot-starter-logging,
             pulled in transitively by every starter here); having the real commons-logging.jar
             on the classpath alongside it is what triggers spring-jcl's own
             "please remove commons-logging.jar from classpath" warning on every startup. -->
        <exclusion>
          <groupId>commons-logging</groupId>
          <artifactId>commons-logging</artifactId>
        </exclusion>
      </exclusions>
    </dependency>

    <!-- PDF statement import (Milestone 1; digital/text-based PDFs only, see
         com.finora.imports.pdf's package doc for scope). Position-aware text extraction
         (TextPosition x/y coordinates), not the library's default plain-text stripper; see
         PdfTextExtractor's own doc comment for why: naive text extraction discards which column
         a piece of text belongs to, which is exactly what distinguishes a debit from a credit
         amount in a real bank statement table. The position-aware API (TextPosition,
         PDFTextStripper.writeString override) has been stable across the whole 3.0.x line.

         3.0.3 (Apr 2024) to 3.0.8 (Jun 2026). NOT a CVE fix, and it is worth being exact about
         that because the obvious assumption is wrong: the only advisories on the 3.0.x line
         (CVE-2026-23907 and CVE-2026-33929, both path traversal in ExtractEmbeddedFiles) are
         against org.apache.pdfbox:pdfbox-examples, which this project does not depend on. No
         published CVE affected the core artifact at 3.0.3.

         The reason to move is the unglamorous half of the same problem. This library parses
         attacker-supplied files as a core product feature, reachable by any authenticated user,
         and 3.0.4 through 3.0.8 are largely hardening against malformed input: stack overflows in
         BaseParser, PDFStreamParser and PDPageTree (PDFBOX-6038/6040/6041), OOM in XrefStreamParser,
         Type1Lexer and PfbParser (PDFBOX-6037/6043/6044), OOM during text extraction
         (PDFBOX-6146), and an infinite loop in CMapParser (PDFBOX-5972). Each of those is a
         crash-or-hang an uploaded file can reach; none carried a CVE number.

         Extraction parity was verified rather than assumed before this bump; see
         PdfTextExtractorSnapshotTest, which exists because of it. Three changes in 3.0.4+ land
         directly on this project's code path: removeContainedSpaces (PDFBOX-5487) runs only in
         the sortByPosition branch, which PdfTextExtractor enables; /ActualText marked content now
         rewrites a TextPosition's unicode; and font-change detection switched from instance
         identity to font name, feeding the heuristic that decides where one writeString run ends
         and the next begins. Output was byte-identical across all 17 committed PDF fixtures
         anyway, but none of that was predictable from the changelog. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.apache.pdfbox</groupId>
      <artifactId>pdfbox</artifactId>
      <version>3.0.8</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Sanitizes merchant receipt HTML before any parser sees it (C5, gmail-transaction-sync
         proposal §12.4). OWASP's sanitizer over jsoup's Safelist because it is allowlist-only by
         construction and has no "unsafe" escape hatch to misconfigure: a receipt parser has no
         reason to need one, and every alternative that supports rich embedding also supports
         turning that support off correctly, which is a mistake to design out rather than avoid.
         Version is a date-stamped release rather than semver; 20260101.1 fixes a noscript/style
         sanitization bypass (GHSA-h5xf-fjfp-cjm2) present in the 20240325.1 release originally
         pinned here. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.googlecode.owasp-java-html-sanitizer</groupId>
      <artifactId>owasp-java-html-sanitizer</artifactId>
      <version>20260101.1</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Cloudflare R2 speaks the S3 API, so the official AWS SDK is the client; there is no
         Cloudflare-specific SDK to prefer, and R2's own documentation points at exactly this.

         apache-client is pinned explicitly rather than left to the default. The SDK otherwise
         resolves netty-nio-client, which pulls a Netty stack this application does not otherwise
         have and does not need for a handful of user-initiated object reads and writes. Excluding
         it keeps the dependency surface and the startup cost down. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
      <artifactId>s3</artifactId>
      <version>2.54.1</version>
      <exclusions>
        <exclusion>
          <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
          <artifactId>netty-nio-client</artifactId>
        </exclusion>
      </exclusions>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>software.amazon.awssdk</groupId>
      <artifactId>apache-client</artifactId>
      <version>2.54.1</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Firebase Admin SDK: server-side verification of Firebase ID tokens issued by Firebase
         Phone Authentication (the frontend's own client-side SDK handles OTP send/verify directly
         against Firebase; this is only what lets the backend trust the resulting token instead of
         taking the frontend's word for it). Replaces this app's own OTP generation/storage/
         verification (see FirebaseConfig's own doc comment for why). Version unverified against
         Maven Central in this sandbox (no network here), same caveat as the pdfbox dependency
         above; bump to the latest 9.x if this specific patch doesn't resolve. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.google.firebase</groupId>
      <artifactId>firebase-admin</artifactId>
      <version>9.10.0</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Utilities -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
      <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
      <optional>true</optional>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Test -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.security</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-security-test</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <!-- NOT test-scoped, and that is the whole point of this comment.

         firebase-admin needs this at RUNTIME: FirebaseOptions.Builder.build() reaches
         ApiClientUtils.getDefaultTransport(), which loads
         org.apache.hc.client5.http.nio.AsyncClientConnectionManager from this artifact. It arrives
         transitively from firebase-admin at compile scope, but Maven resolves scope by nearest
         definition, so a DIRECT declaration here overrides that transitive one. Declaring it with
         scope `test` therefore did not "add it for tests"; it REMOVED it from the runtime
         classpath and from the Spring Boot fat jar.

         That took production down on 2026-08-08: every boot failed with NoClassDefFoundError on
         that class, through FirebaseConfig.firebaseApp into productionConfigValidator, before the
         web server ever started. The test suite could not have caught it, because CI runs
         `mvn test`, where the artifact is present either way, while the image is built by
         `mvn package`, where it was not. The gap stayed invisible for four days: firebase-admin
         landed 2026-08-02 and the test-scoped declaration landed 2026-08-04, so every jar built
         after that shipped without it and nothing restarted until the next deploy.

         Keep it unscoped. It still satisfies the test usage it was originally added for: Spring
         Boot makes TestRestTemplate use Apache HttpClient rather than the JDK's
         HttpURLConnection once this is on the classpath, which AuthFlowIT's CORS test depends on
         (HttpURLConnection silently drops "restricted" request headers, Origin among them, so that
         test asserted on a request the JDK had already stripped) and which its wrong-password test
         depends on too (HttpURLConnection throws HttpRetryException instead of surfacing a plain
         401). Both are client limitations, not application behaviour. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.apache.httpcomponents.client5</groupId>
      <artifactId>httpclient5</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>testcontainers</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.testcontainers</groupId>
      <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <!-- Architecture tests: encode invariants (e.g. "every admin-path controller method is
         @PreAuthorize-guarded") as a reusable diagnostic that fails the build the next time the
         invariant is violated, instead of relying on another manual audit to catch it. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>com.tngtech.archunit</groupId>
      <artifactId>archunit-junit5</artifactId>
      <version>1.5.0</version>
      <scope>test</scope>
    </dependency>
    <!-- Backend error monitoring. The three client apps have had Sentry since their own
         monitoring.ts landed; the tier that runs the import pipeline, the async workers and the
         queues had none, so a production exception there was visible only in application logs.

         The jakarta starter is the Spring Boot 3 variant (javax to jakarta namespace). Version is
         pinned rather than inherited: spring-boot-starter-parent does not manage Sentry, so an
         unpinned range would move on its own.

         Disabled unless SENTRY_DSN is set; see MonitoringConfig and application.yml. Absent
         config degrades to a no-op, matching every other external integration here. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.sentry</groupId>
      <artifactId>sentry-spring-boot-starter-jakarta</artifactId>
      <version>8.53.0</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Metrics export. Micrometer core already arrives with spring-boot-starter-actuator; this
         adds only the Prometheus scrape format, so worker counters, timers and queue-depth gauges
         become dashboard-able instead of visible only in a debugger.

         Version is managed by spring-boot-starter-parent, so it is not pinned here. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
      <artifactId>micrometer-registry-prometheus</artifactId>
    </dependency>

  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <!-- Generates META-INF/build-info.properties, which Spring Boot auto-detects and
               exposes as a BuildProperties bean, backing Platform Diagnostics' "Application
               Version" and build-time fields. No new dependency, this goal ships with the plugin
               already on the classpath. -->
          <execution>
            <goals>
              <goal>build-info</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
        <configuration>
          <excludes>
            <exclude>
              <groupId>org.projectlombok</groupId>
              <artifactId>lombok</artifactId>
            </exclude>
          </excludes>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
      <plugin>
        <!-- Generates git.properties on the classpath, auto-detected by Spring Boot as a
             GitProperties bean, backing Platform Diagnostics' "Git Commit" field. Both this and
             build-info above are read via ObjectProvider (see AdminDiagnosticsService) so a
             build run without Maven (e.g. an IDE's own compiler) that skips these goals doesn't
             break startup; the fields just report as unavailable rather than the app failing
             to boot. -->
        <groupId>io.github.git-commit-id</groupId>
        <artifactId>git-commit-id-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <goals>
              <goal>revision</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
        <configuration>
          <generateGitPropertiesFile>true</generateGitPropertiesFile>
          <!-- This is a scaffold started fresh, possibly outside any git repo yet; fail the
               build only if git metadata is genuinely expected (CI can override this property),
               not on every local dev build before a first commit exists. -->
          <failOnNoGitDirectory>false</failOnNoGitDirectory>
          <failOnUnableToExtractRepoInfo>false</failOnUnableToExtractRepoInfo>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
      <plugin>
        <!-- Two things, both of which existed only by accident before.

             1. INCLUDES. The 29 *IT classes in src/test had never run. Surefire's defaults are
                Test*.java, *Test.java, *Tests.java and *TestCase.java; *IT.java is the
                maven-failsafe convention and failsafe was never configured. So every integration
                test here (AuthFlowIT, AdminRbacIT, PasswordChangeFlowIT, GlobalAuditLogIT,
                TransactionRepositoryIT, all 21 admin controller ITs) compiled, looked like coverage
                in review, and asserted nothing anywhere. It is what made com.finora.controller
                report 0.0% the moment JaCoCo was added, and it made ci.yml's claim that the suite
                includes "the Testcontainers ones" untrue.

                Fixed by widening surefire's includes rather than adding failsafe and a separate
                `verify` phase. AbstractIntegrationTest's doc comment describes one container shared
                across "the full suite", CI runs `./mvnw test` and expects Docker for exactly that
                reason, and the README says the same. Splitting them into a phase nobody runs would
                keep the documentation wrong. The trade is that `./mvnw test` genuinely needs a
                Docker daemon now, which was already the documented expectation.

             2. api.version. See the docker.api.version property for why. It has to be passed to the
                forked test JVM as a system property; setting it in the parent shell does nothing.

             3. spring.profiles.active. application.yml resolves this as
                `${SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE:dev}` — nothing in this pom or in ci.yml's "Run tests"
                step ever set that variable, so every test run, in CI and locally, has always
                booted under the "dev" profile. application-test.yml — which documents itself as
                globally disabling six background schedulers under test (the merchant-learning
                queue, import-session cleanup, the statement-storage sweep, the account-purge
                sweep, audit-log redaction, and Gmail state-cleanup/discovery) — was consequently
                dead configuration: it never actually loaded. Those schedulers ran live, on their
                real default intervals, against the same shared Testcontainers Postgres every IT
                class uses, for the whole life of the suite. Confirmed empirically, not assumed: a
                live "scheduling-1" thread was caught mid-suite calling
                MerchantLearningEventWorker.recoverAbandoned(), which only a genuinely enabled,
                unmocked scheduler can do, and every context-boot log line in that run reported
                "profile is active: dev", none "test". That is what made MerchantLearningQueueIT
                intermittently flaky under the full suite (never in isolation, where no other IT
                class exists to bring up a live, default-configured worker) and put every other
                test relying on the same documented-but-inert defaults at identical risk. Set here,
                as a system property for the same reason api.version is: this is what the forked
                test JVM actually reads, not the parent shell's environment. -->
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <includes>
            <include>**/Test*.java</include>
            <include>**/*Test.java</include>
            <include>**/*Tests.java</include>
            <include>**/*TestCase.java</include>
            <include>**/*IT.java</include>
          </includes>
          <systemPropertyVariables>
            <api.version>${docker.api.version}</api.version>
            <spring.profiles.active>test</spring.profiles.active>
          </systemPropertyVariables>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
      <plugin>
        <!-- Coverage measurement. The suite is large and passing, but until now what fraction of
             the code it actually exercises was simply unknown, and test COUNT is not the signal it
             looks like: the rate-limit bypass fixed in 6ee925a had five dedicated tests that all
             passed while the filter was bypassable, because every one of them fed it an
             already-canonical path. Those lines were covered; the inputs were wrong.

             So this is deliberately a REPORT, not a gate. No `check` goal, no minimum threshold,
             nothing that can fail the build. A coverage number invites a coverage target, and
             targets produce tests written to move the number rather than to catch bugs, which is
             the opposite of how every guard in this codebase (OwnershipGuardUsageTest,
             MoneyComparisonUsageTest, FilterPathMatchingTest) was written. What this is for is
             answering "where is there no net at all", which a count cannot answer.

             Bound to `test` rather than `verify` so that `./mvnw test`, the command CI and
             everyone here already runs, produces target/site/jacoco/index.html with no extra
             step.

             WHAT IT FOUND ON ITS FIRST RUN. 78.3% instructions overall, but com.finora.controller
             at 0.0% across 1700 instructions. That was not a coverage gap: controllers are only
             exercised by the *IT classes, and all 29 of those had never executed, because *IT did
             not match surefire's default includes. Adding them (see the surefire plugin above)
             took the suite from 941 tests to 1055 and lifted coverage to 88.0% instructions /
             78.0% branches, with com.finora.controller going from 0.0% to 63.0%.

             Which is the argument for this plugin existing. A passing count of 941 looked like
             health; it was hiding a third of the test suite never running at all. -->
        <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
        <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>0.8.15</version>
        <executions>
          <execution>
            <id>prepare-agent</id>
            <goals>
              <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
          <execution>
            <id>report</id>
            <phase>test</phase>
            <goals>
              <goal>report</goal>
            </goals>
          </execution>
        </executions>
        <configuration>
          <excludes>
            <!-- Generated or structural code with no behaviour to cover. Excluded so the number
                 reflects logic that could actually be wrong, rather than being diluted by
                 accessors and enum boilerplate. -->
            <exclude>com/finora/dto/**</exclude>
            <exclude>com/finora/entity/**</exclude>
            <exclude>com/finora/FinoraApplication.class</exclude>
          </excludes>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
